INSERT INTO public.lessons (course_id, module_id, title, position, type, order_index, is_free, content) VALUES

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m1', 'What Is Artificial Intelligence?', 1, 'reading', 1, true,
E'## What Is Artificial Intelligence?\n\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is software that can do things that normally require human intelligence — like understanding language, recognising images, writing text, or making decisions.\n\n---\n\n### AI is already part of your daily life\n\nYou probably use AI every day without thinking about it:\n\n- **Google Search** predicts what you''re looking for before you finish typing\n- **Netflix and Spotify** recommend what to watch or listen to next\n- **WhatsApp** autocorrects your spelling\n- **Gmail** filters spam out of your inbox\n- **Maps** finds the fastest route in real time\n\n---\n\n### The three types of AI you should know\n\n**1. Narrow AI (what exists today)**\nDesigned to do one specific task extremely well. A chess-playing AI beats world champions — but it can''t make you a cup of tea. This is the only type of AI that currently exists.\n\n**2. Generative AI**\nA newer category of narrow AI that can *create* — it can write text, generate images, write code, and more. Examples: ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Midjourney.\n\n**3. Artificial General Intelligence (AGI)**\nA theoretical future AI that could perform any intellectual task a human can. This does *not* exist yet, despite what headlines sometimes suggest.\n\n---\n\n### How does generative AI actually work?\n\nThink of it like a very well-read assistant. Generative AI models are trained on massive amounts of text from the internet, books, and other sources. Through this training they learn patterns — how ideas connect, how sentences are structured, how problems are solved.\n\nWhen you ask it a question, it predicts the most useful and relevant response based on everything it''s learned.\n\n> **Important:** AI doesn''t "think" the way humans do. It doesn''t have feelings, opinions, or consciousness. It''s a powerful tool — and like any tool, how useful it is depends on how well you use it.\n\n---\n\n### Why does this matter for your career?\n\nAI is changing almost every industry. Employers are increasingly expecting workers to:\n- Understand what AI can and can''t do\n- Use AI tools to work faster and smarter\n- Know when *not* to trust AI output\n\nYou don''t need to be a programmer to benefit from AI. You need to be comfortable using it — and that''s exactly what this module is about.\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ AI is software that performs tasks requiring human-like intelligence\n✅ Generative AI (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini) can write, code, and create\n✅ AI doesn''t think — it predicts based on patterns\n✅ Understanding AI gives you a competitive advantage in the workplace'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m1', 'AI Tools You Can Use Right Now', 2, 'reading', 2, true,
E'## AI Tools You Can Use Right Now\n\nYou don''t need to build AI — you need to use it. Here are the most useful AI tools available today, most of them free.\n\n---\n\n### The big three AI assistants\n\n| Tool | Made by | Best for | Free tier? |\n|------|---------|----------|------------|\n| **ChatGPT** | OpenAI | Writing, research, coding, brainstorming | Yes |\n| **Claude** | Anthropic | Long documents, analysis, nuanced writing | Yes |\n| **Gemini** | Google | Search integration, Google Workspace tasks | Yes |\n\nAll three work the same basic way: you type a message (called a **prompt**), and the AI responds. The better your prompt, the better the answer.\n\n---\n\n### How to write a good prompt\n\nMost people get poor results from AI because they write vague prompts. Compare these two:\n\n❌ **Weak:** "Help me with my CV"\n\n✅ **Strong:** "I''m a 22-year-old recent graduate applying for my first customer service role. I have no formal work experience but I have 6 months of volunteer experience at a community centre and completed a WIL placement at a logistics company. Write a professional CV summary paragraph for me."\n\nThe key ingredients of a good prompt:\n1. **Context** — who you are and what situation you''re in\n2. **Task** — exactly what you want it to do\n3. **Format** — how you want the output (bullet list, paragraph, table, etc.)\n4. **Constraints** — any limits (word count, tone, what to avoid)\n\n---\n\n### Practical uses at work\n\n**Writing and communication**\n- Draft professional emails ("Write a polite follow-up email to a recruiter I met last week")\n- Summarise long documents ("Summarise this 20-page report in 5 bullet points")\n- Improve your own writing ("Make this paragraph sound more professional")\n\n**Research and learning**\n- Get quick explanations ("Explain supply chain management to me like I''m a beginner")\n- Compare options ("What are the pros and cons of Excel vs Google Sheets?")\n- Prepare for tasks ("What questions should I ask in my first performance review?")\n\n**Problem solving**\n- Debug issues ("Here is my error message — what does it mean?")\n- Brainstorm ideas ("Give me 10 ideas for improving customer satisfaction in a call centre")\n- Plan projects ("Help me create a 30-60-90 day plan for starting a new job")\n\n---\n\n### Free tools worth bookmarking\n\n- **chat.openai.com** — ChatGPT\n- **claude.ai** — Claude\n- **gemini.google.com** — Gemini\n- **grammarly.com** — AI writing assistant\n- **canva.com** — AI-powered design (great for CVs and presentations)\n- **otter.ai** — AI meeting notes\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini are the most useful free AI tools available\n✅ The quality of your prompt directly determines the quality of the output\n✅ AI can help you write, research, summarise, and problem-solve faster\n✅ You don''t need to pay — free tiers are powerful enough for most tasks'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m1', 'Using AI Responsibly and Critically', 3, 'reading', 3, true,
E'## Using AI Responsibly and Critically\n\nAI is a powerful tool — but it can also produce incorrect, biased, or inappropriate content. This lesson teaches you how to use AI in a way that protects your reputation and keeps you out of trouble.\n\n---\n\n### The biggest mistake people make with AI\n\n**Trusting it blindly.**\n\nAI can be confidently wrong. It may produce facts that sound convincing but are completely made up. This is called a **hallucination** — the AI generates plausible-sounding but false information.\n\n**Example:** You ask ChatGPT to list 5 academic studies about employee burnout. It confidently gives you titles, author names, and journal references — but some of them simply don''t exist.\n\n> **Rule:** Always verify important facts, statistics, and references from AI using real sources.\n\n---\n\n### What AI is good at vs what it''s bad at\n\n| AI is good at | AI is bad at |\n|---|---|\n| Drafting and editing text | Providing accurate real-time information |\n| Brainstorming ideas | Knowing what happened recently (training cutoff) |\n| Explaining concepts | Citing sources reliably |\n| Summarising documents | Understanding your specific context deeply |\n| Writing code | Replacing human judgment on complex decisions |\n\n---\n\n### Privacy: what you should never type into AI\n\nFree AI tools send your prompts to servers run by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic. That data may be used to train future models.\n\n**Never paste into a public AI tool:**\n- Passwords or login credentials\n- Your company''s confidential documents or client data\n- Personal identification numbers (ID, passport, banking)\n- Private medical or legal information\n- Your employer''s trade secrets or financial data\n\n> Many companies have strict policies about what employees may share with external AI tools. When in doubt, ask your manager.\n\n---\n\n### Academic and workplace honesty\n\nUsing AI to complete work that is meant to be your own — without disclosing it — is dishonest. This includes:\n- Submitting AI-written assignments as your own\n- Using AI in assessments that prohibit it\n- Misrepresenting AI-assisted work to your employer\n\n**Using AI as a tool is fine. Pretending you did the work yourself when you didn''t is not.**\n\nBest practice: use AI to help you *think and draft*, then rewrite in your own voice. The final work should reflect your understanding.\n\n---\n\n### Three questions to ask before using AI output\n\n1. **Is this accurate?** Can I verify this claim independently?\n2. **Is this appropriate?** Does this output fit the context — tone, audience, sensitivity?\n3. **Is this mine?** Have I understood, reviewed, and taken ownership of this content?\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ AI can hallucinate — always verify important facts\n✅ Never paste confidential or personal data into public AI tools\n✅ Using AI to help is fine; submitting AI work as entirely your own is dishonest\n✅ Strong AI users critically evaluate output rather than copying it directly');

