INSERT INTO public.lessons (course_id, module_id, title, position, type, order_index, is_free, content) VALUES

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m2', 'What Employers Actually Look For in a CV', 1, 'reading', 4, true,
E'## What Employers Actually Look For in a CV\n\nBefore you write a single word, you need to understand who you''re writing for. Your CV is not a life story — it''s a targeted marketing document. Its only job is to get you an interview.\n\n---\n\n### How long do recruiters spend on a CV?\n\nStudies consistently show that recruiters spend an average of **6–10 seconds** on an initial CV scan. In that time, they''re looking for three things:\n\n1. **Can this person do the job?** (skills and experience)\n2. **Are they serious?** (presentation and professionalism)\n3. **Do they understand the role?** (relevance to the position)\n\nIf the answer to all three looks promising, the CV goes into the "yes" pile for a closer read.\n\n---\n\n### The 5 sections every strong CV includes\n\n**1. Contact Information**\nName, professional email address, phone number, city (not full address), and optionally LinkedIn profile URL.\n\n❌ jacktheboss2003@gmail.com\n✅ jack.mokoena@gmail.com\n\n**2. Professional Summary (2–4 sentences)**\nA snapshot of who you are professionally and what you bring. Written in third person or first person — be consistent.\n\n*Example for a recent graduate:*\n> Motivated BCom graduate with a strong foundation in financial analysis and customer service. Completed a 6-month WIL placement at a logistics firm where I developed Excel reporting dashboards that reduced weekly reporting time by 30%. Seeking a junior analyst role where I can contribute to data-driven decision making.\n\n**3. Education**\nDegree/qualification, institution, year completed or expected. Include relevant modules if you''re a recent graduate.\n\n**4. Work Experience / Internships / WIL**\nList in reverse chronological order (most recent first). For each role include:\n- Company name, your title, dates\n- 3–5 bullet points starting with action verbs\n- Focus on what you *achieved*, not just what you *did*\n\n❌ "Responsible for answering calls"\n✅ "Resolved an average of 40+ customer queries daily, maintaining a 95% satisfaction rating"\n\n**5. Skills**\nTechnical skills (software, tools, languages) and soft skills. Be honest — if you list Excel, expect to be tested on it.\n\n---\n\n### What makes a CV immediately rejected\n\n- Spelling or grammar mistakes\n- Generic summaries ("hardworking team player")\n- Photos in most professional contexts\n- Unexplained gaps without context\n- Font sizes smaller than 10pt or larger than 12pt for body text\n- Longer than 2 pages for early-career candidates\n- An unprofessional email address\n\n---\n\n### ATS: The robot that reads before humans do\n\nLarge companies use **Applicant Tracking Systems (ATS)** — software that scans CVs for keywords before a human ever sees them. If your CV doesn''t include keywords from the job description, it may never reach a recruiter.\n\n**Tips to beat ATS:**\n- Mirror language from the job posting\n- Use standard section headings (not creative alternatives like "My Journey")\n- Avoid tables, text boxes, and columns — they confuse ATS scanners\n- Save and send as PDF unless the application specifies otherwise\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Recruiters spend 6–10 seconds on a first scan — make it count\n✅ Every section should answer: "Why should they hire me?"\n✅ Use action verbs and show achievements, not just responsibilities\n✅ Tailor your CV to each job using keywords from the job description'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m2', 'Building Your CV Step by Step', 2, 'reading', 5, true,
E'## Building Your CV Step by Step\n\nIn this lesson you''ll build your CV section by section using a clear structure. By the end, you''ll have a complete first draft ready to refine.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Contact section\n\nKeep it clean and professional at the top of page one.\n\n```\nThandeka Dlamini\nthandeka.dlamini@gmail.com | 071 234 5678 | Cape Town, WC\nlinkedin.com/in/thandekadlamini\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Professional summary\n\nWrite 2–4 sentences that answer:\n- Who are you professionally?\n- What''s your strongest relevant experience or skill?\n- What are you looking for?\n\nPrompt you can use with AI:\n> *"I''m a [qualification] graduate from [institution]. I completed a WIL placement at [company] where I [key achievement]. I also have experience in [skill/activity]. Write a 3-sentence professional CV summary for a [target role] position."*\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Education\n\nRecent graduate format:\n\n```\nBachelor of Commerce — Business Management\nUniversity of the Western Cape | 2021–2024\nRelevant modules: Business Strategy, Financial Accounting, Marketing Principles\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Experience — the most important section\n\nEven if you have no formal jobs, you have experience. Include:\n- WIL / internship placements\n- Part-time or casual work\n- Volunteer work\n- Freelance or community projects\n- Student leadership roles\n\n**Format each entry like this:**\n\n```\nCustomer Service Intern\nRetail Company XYZ | Jan 2024 – Jun 2024\n• Assisted an average of 60 customers daily across in-store and phone channels\n• Processed returns and exchanges following company policy with zero escalations\n• Trained 2 new temporary staff members during peak season\n• Recognised as "Intern of the Month" in March 2024\n```\n\n**Action verbs to use:**\nAchieved · Analysed · Built · Collaborated · Coordinated · Created · Delivered · Designed · Developed · Improved · Led · Managed · Organised · Presented · Reduced · Resolved · Supported · Trained\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Skills section\n\nGroup by type:\n\n```\nTechnical Skills: Microsoft Excel (Intermediate), Google Workspace, Canva, ChatGPT\nSoft Skills: Written communication, time management, customer service\nLanguages: English (fluent), Zulu (conversational)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Optional sections\n\nAdd these if you have them:\n- **Certifications** (NobzLearn, Google, Microsoft, etc.)\n- **Awards & Achievements**\n- **Extracurricular Activities** (especially leadership roles)\n- **References** — "Available on request" is fine unless asked\n\n---\n\n### The 5-minute CV checklist before you submit\n\n- [ ] No spelling or grammar errors\n- [ ] Professional email address\n- [ ] Consistent font (Arial, Calibri, or Helvetica — 11pt body)\n- [ ] All dates are correct\n- [ ] File saved as PDF and named: `FirstName_LastName_CV.pdf`\n- [ ] Under 2 pages\n- [ ] Every bullet starts with an action verb\n- [ ] No photo (unless specifically requested)\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Structure your CV: Contact → Summary → Education → Experience → Skills\n✅ Use action verbs and quantify achievements wherever possible\n✅ WIL, volunteering, and part-time work all count as experience\n✅ Always save as PDF named with your full name'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m2', 'Using AI to Write and Improve Your CV', 3, 'reading', 6, true,
E'## Using AI to Write and Improve Your CV\n\nAI tools can dramatically speed up the CV writing process — but only if you use them strategically. This lesson shows you exactly how.\n\n---\n\n### What AI is great at for CVs\n\n- Turning rough notes into polished bullet points\n- Rewriting weak phrases into strong, action-oriented language\n- Tailoring your CV summary to a specific job\n- Identifying keywords in a job posting\n- Proofreading and suggesting improvements\n- Formatting advice\n\n---\n\n### Prompt templates you can use today\n\n**1. Write my professional summary**\n> *"I am a [qualification] graduate from [institution], graduating in [year]. My WIL placement was at [company] in [role], where I [brief description of what you did]. I also have [other experience]. Write a 3-sentence professional CV summary for someone applying for [target role]."*\n\n**2. Turn a job duty into a strong bullet point**\n> *"Rewrite this CV bullet point to sound more professional and achievement-focused, starting with a strong action verb: ''I was responsible for customer complaints.'' Also suggest 2 alternative versions."*\n\n**3. Tailor your CV to a specific job**\n> *"Here is a job description: [paste job description]. Here is my current CV summary: [paste your summary]. Rewrite my summary to better match this job, using keywords from the job description without making it dishonest."*\n\n**4. Identify keywords from a job ad**\n> *"Read this job posting and list the 10 most important keywords I should include in my CV to pass an ATS screening: [paste job posting]."*\n\n**5. Proofread and improve**\n> *"Review this CV section for grammar, spelling, and professionalism. Suggest improvements but keep my voice: [paste section]."*\n\n---\n\n### The AI-to-human review process\n\nNever paste AI output directly onto your CV without reviewing it. Follow this process:\n\n1. **Generate** — use the prompts above to create a draft\n2. **Read** — read it out loud. Does it sound like you?\n3. **Edit** — change any phrases that feel unnatural or inaccurate\n4. **Verify** — make sure every claim reflects something real you did\n5. **Polish** — run it through Grammarly for a final grammar check\n\n---\n\n### Red flags: what AI sometimes gets wrong\n\n| AI mistake | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| Invents achievements you don''t have | Delete or replace with real ones |\n| Uses buzzwords that sound hollow | Rewrite in plain, specific language |\n| Makes you sound more senior than you are | Adjust tone to match your level |\n| Produces generic phrases ("results-driven") | Ask it to be more specific |\n\n---\n\n### Tools to use\n\n- **ChatGPT / Claude / Gemini** — prompting and drafting (free)\n- **Grammarly** — grammar and spelling (free tier is strong)\n- **Canva** — clean CV templates that look professional (free)\n- **LinkedIn** — benchmark your CV against people in your target role\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Use specific prompts — vague prompts produce vague CVs\n✅ Always review and personalise AI-generated content before submitting\n✅ Use AI to tailor your CV to each specific job posting\n✅ Grammarly + AI + your own review = a clean, professional CV');

