INSERT INTO public.lessons (course_id, module_id, title, position, type, order_index, is_free, content) VALUES

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m3', 'Preparing for a Job Interview', 1, 'reading', 7, true,
E'## Preparing for a Job Interview\n\nThe candidates who perform best in interviews are almost never the most talented. They''re the most prepared. This lesson gives you a step-by-step system for interview preparation.\n\n---\n\n### Why preparation matters\n\nInterviewers notice the difference between a candidate who has researched the company and one who hasn''t within the first two minutes. Preparation shows:\n- You take the opportunity seriously\n- You understand what the role requires\n- You can connect your experience to their specific needs\n\n---\n\n### Your 48-hour preparation checklist\n\n**Research the company (1–2 hours)**\n- [ ] What does the company do? What products or services do they offer?\n- [ ] Who are their main customers or clients?\n- [ ] What are their core values or mission statement?\n- [ ] Have they been in the news recently? (Google "[Company name] news 2024")\n- [ ] What do employees say about working there? (Check Glassdoor)\n\n**Understand the role (30 minutes)**\n- [ ] What are the key responsibilities listed in the job description?\n- [ ] What skills are they emphasising?\n- [ ] What does a typical day in this role look like?\n\n**Prepare your answers (2–3 hours)**\n- [ ] Practice the most common questions (covered in the next lesson)\n- [ ] Prepare 3–5 STAR stories from your experience (covered below)\n- [ ] Write down 3–5 questions to ask them\n\n**Practical preparation (day before)**\n- [ ] Confirm the interview time, format (in-person or video), and location/link\n- [ ] Prepare your outfit (professional, clean, ironed)\n- [ ] If in-person: plan your route and add 20 minutes buffer for transport\n- [ ] If video: test your camera, microphone, and internet connection\n- [ ] Print 2 copies of your CV\n\n---\n\n### The STAR method for answering questions\n\nMany interview questions ask you to describe a past experience. Use STAR to structure your answer:\n\n**S — Situation:** Set the scene briefly. Where were you? What was the context?\n**T — Task:** What was your responsibility or challenge?\n**A — Action:** What did YOU specifically do? (Use "I", not "we")\n**R — Result:** What was the outcome? Be specific if possible.\n\n**Example question:** "Tell me about a time you had to deal with a difficult customer."\n\n**Weak answer:** "I always try to stay calm and help customers as best I can."\n\n**STAR answer:**\n*Situation:* "During my WIL placement at a retail store, a customer came in very angry about a product that had stopped working after one week."\n*Task:* "As the only staff member on the floor at the time, I needed to resolve the complaint while keeping the customer in the store."\n*Action:* "I listened without interrupting, apologised sincerely, checked our returns policy, and offered a full exchange. When the replacement was out of stock, I offered a rain check and a 10% discount on their next purchase."\n*Result:* "The customer left satisfied and actually returned the following week to say thank you. My supervisor mentioned the incident in my end-of-placement review as an example of strong customer handling."\n\n---\n\n### 3 questions to always prepare to ask\n\nAlways have questions ready — "I don''t have any questions" signals disinterest.\n\nGood questions to ask:\n1. *"What does success look like in this role after the first 90 days?"*\n2. *"What''s the team culture like — how do people typically collaborate?"*\n3. *"What are the biggest challenges the person in this role will face?"*\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Research the company before every interview — it''s noticed\n✅ Use STAR to structure your experience-based answers\n✅ Always prepare 3–5 questions to ask the interviewer\n✅ Prepare practically: outfit, route, and tech the day before'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m3', 'Common Interview Questions and How to Answer Them', 2, 'reading', 8, true,
E'## Common Interview Questions and How to Answer Them\n\nMost interviews follow a predictable pattern. Knowing the most common questions — and how to answer them well — removes most of the uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n### "Tell me about yourself."\n\nThis is almost always the first question. It''s not an invitation to recite your CV. It''s your 60–90 second commercial.\n\n**Structure:**\n1. Where you are now (qualification/recent role)\n2. What you''ve done that''s relevant (1–2 key achievements)\n3. Why you''re here (why this role/company)\n\n**Example:**\n> "I''m a recent BCom graduate from Cape Peninsula University of Technology, where I majored in Human Resources. During my WIL placement at a logistics company, I supported the HR team in onboarding over 40 seasonal workers — including preparing contracts and running orientation sessions. I''m passionate about building positive work cultures and I''m drawn to this role because your company''s focus on employee development aligns strongly with my career goals."\n\n---\n\n### "What are your strengths?"\n\nDon''t just list traits. Pick 2–3 relevant strengths and give a brief example of each.\n\n❌ "I''m hardworking, reliable, and a good communicator."\n\n✅ "My strongest skill is written communication — during my internship I was asked to take over the company newsletter because my manager said my writing was clearer than the previous version. I also tend to be very organised; I use a system of daily priorities that helped me meet every deadline during a particularly busy period."\n\n---\n\n### "What are your weaknesses?"\n\nBe honest, but choose a real weakness that you''re actively improving — and don''t pick something critical to the role.\n\n❌ "I work too hard." (Sounds fake)\n❌ "I''m not very good with people." (Disqualifying for most roles)\n\n✅ "I used to find it difficult to ask for help when I was stuck — I''d spend too long trying to figure things out alone. I''ve worked on this by setting a personal rule: if I haven''t made progress in 30 minutes, I flag it with my supervisor. I''ve found it actually speeds up my work significantly."\n\n---\n\n### "Why do you want to work here?"\n\nThis is where your research pays off. Reference something specific about the company — not just "you''re a great company."\n\n✅ "I''ve been following your company''s expansion into community-based financial services. The work you''re doing in underserved areas aligns with why I studied finance — I want to work where it creates meaningful impact. I also read that your graduate programme includes mentorship from senior leaders, which is exactly the kind of environment I learn best in."\n\n---\n\n### "Where do you see yourself in 5 years?"\n\nDon''t say "in your position" (awkward) or "I don''t know" (unprepared). Show ambition while staying realistic.\n\n✅ "In five years I''d like to be a confident mid-level professional in HR, ideally specialising in talent development. I want to deepen my expertise over the next few years through hands-on experience and structured learning — I see this role as a strong first step in that direction."\n\n---\n\n### Behavioural questions — common examples\n\nThese all start with "Tell me about a time when..." or "Give me an example of..."\n\n| Question | What they''re really testing |\n|---|---|\n| "Tell me about a time you missed a deadline." | Accountability and recovery |\n| "Describe a conflict with a colleague." | Emotional intelligence |\n| "Tell me about a time you had to learn something quickly." | Adaptability |\n| "Give an example of taking initiative." | Drive and ownership |\n| "Describe a situation where you had to work under pressure." | Composure and prioritisation |\n\n**For all of these: use STAR.**\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ "Tell me about yourself" is a 60-second pitch, not a life story\n✅ Pair every strength and weakness with a real example\n✅ Reference specific company research in your "Why us?" answer\n✅ Use STAR for all behavioural questions'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m3', 'Interview Confidence and Body Language', 3, 'reading', 9, true,
E'## Interview Confidence and Body Language\n\nResearch by communication experts shows that in face-to-face conversations, non-verbal communication carries enormous weight. In interviews, how you come across physically matters almost as much as what you say.\n\n---\n\n### Before you walk in\n\n**Manage nerves beforehand:**\n- Do a slow breath: inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 4, exhale for 6. Repeat 3 times.\n- Arrive 10 minutes early — rushing makes nerves worse\n- Remember: the interviewer wants you to do well. They''re hoping you''re the right person.\n\n**Power posing (optional but evidence-based):**\nStand in a confident posture — feet apart, hands on hips, chin up — for 2 minutes before walking in. Research suggests it increases feelings of confidence.\n\n---\n\n### Non-verbal signals that build trust\n\n**Eye contact**\nMaintain natural eye contact — not a stare, but consistent engagement. Look away briefly when thinking, then return. In a panel interview, direct your answer to whoever asked the question but glance at others too.\n\n**Handshake**\nFirm, one pump, dry. Practice if needed.\n\n**Posture**\nSit upright, feet flat on the floor. Don''t slump or lean back too casually — it reads as disinterest. Don''t sit rigidly either — natural and upright is the goal.\n\n**Facial expression**\nSmile when it''s natural. Nod when the interviewer is speaking to show you''re listening.\n\n**Hands**\nKeep them visible. Using hands to emphasise points is natural and professional — just avoid excessive movement.\n\n---\n\n### What not to do\n\n| Body language | What it signals |\n|---|---|\n| Crossing arms tightly | Defensive or closed off |\n| Looking at the floor | Lack of confidence |\n| Fidgeting (tapping, leg bouncing) | Nervousness or restlessness |\n| Checking your phone | Complete disrespect |\n| Slumping in chair | Disengagement |\n| Touching face or neck often | Anxiety or dishonesty |\n\n---\n\n### For video interviews\n\nVideo interviews add extra considerations:\n\n- **Camera at eye level** — not looking up at the camera from below or down from above\n- **Light in front of you** — not behind (backlighting makes you look like a silhouette)\n- **Clean, neutral background** — a plain wall beats a messy room\n- **Look at the camera** when speaking, not at the interviewer''s face on screen (the camera is eye contact)\n- **Dress professionally from head to toe** — yes, even the parts they can''t see. It affects your mindset.\n- **Test everything** — camera, mic, internet, app — the day before\n\n---\n\n### Ending the interview strongly\n\nThe last impression is almost as important as the first.\n\n- When asked "Do you have any questions?" — ask at least two thoughtful questions\n- Express genuine enthusiasm: *"I''m really excited about this opportunity — the role aligns perfectly with what I''m looking for."\n- Close with gratitude: *"Thank you so much for your time — I look forward to hearing from you."\n- Follow up with a brief thank-you email within 24 hours\n\n**Thank-you email template:**\n> Subject: Thank you — [Role Title] Interview\n>\n> Dear [Interviewer Name],\n>\n> Thank you for taking the time to speak with me today about the [Role Title] position. I enjoyed learning more about [something specific from the conversation] and I''m even more excited about the opportunity after our discussion.\n>\n> I look forward to hearing from you regarding the next steps.\n>\n> Kind regards,\n> [Your Name]\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Body language — posture, eye contact, expression — affects how you''re perceived\n✅ Calm your nerves with controlled breathing before walking in\n✅ In video interviews, look at the camera, not the screen\n✅ Always follow up with a thank-you email within 24 hours');

