-- MODULE 4 — Workplace Conduct & Professionalism
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

INSERT INTO public.lessons (course_id, module_id, title, position, type, order_index, is_free, content) VALUES

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m4', 'Professional Communication at Work', 1, 'reading', 10, true,
E'## Professional Communication at Work\n\nThe biggest surprise most people experience when they enter the workplace is how much communication matters. Technical skills get you the job — communication skills determine how far you go.\n\n---\n\n### The golden rule of professional communication\n\n**Clarity over cleverness.** In a professional setting, the goal is for your message to be understood correctly the first time — not to impress people with vocabulary.\n\n---\n\n### Email: the backbone of workplace communication\n\n**The anatomy of a professional email:**\n\n```\nSubject: [Clear, specific — "Invoice #4521 — Query" not just "Query"]\n\nDear Mr/Ms [Surname], / Hi [First name], (match formality to your relationship)\n\nOpening line — state your purpose in the first sentence.\n"I am writing to follow up on the proposal submitted on 12 June."\n\nBody — keep it brief. One main point per email if possible.\n\nClosing — clear next step or call to action.\n"Please let me know if you require any additional information."\n\nSign-off: Kind regards, / Best regards, / Thank you,\nYour full name\nJob title | Company | Contact number\n```\n\n**Email rules:**\n- Reply within 24 hours, even if just to say you received it and will respond soon\n- Never send an email in anger — write it, save as draft, re-read the next morning\n- CC only people who genuinely need to see the message\n- Do not reply all unless everyone needs to see your response\n- Proofread before sending — typos in emails make you look careless\n\n---\n\n### WhatsApp and instant messaging at work\n\nMany South African workplaces use WhatsApp for internal communication. Different rules apply:\n- Still professional — avoid slang and emoji overuse in work groups\n- Keep messages concise\n- Don''t message colleagues after hours about non-urgent matters unless your workplace culture explicitly does so\n- Group chats are for work — not for personal conversations\n\n---\n\n### Verbal communication in meetings and with senior colleagues\n\n**In meetings:**\n- Arrive on time — lateness without notice is disrespectful\n- Don''t interrupt; raise your hand or wait for a pause\n- Contribute at least once per meeting — silence makes you invisible\n- If you disagree, say it respectfully: *"I see it slightly differently — could I share an alternative view?"*\n\n**With managers and senior colleagues:**\n- Address people by title + surname (Mr Nkosi, Ms van der Berg) until invited to use first names\n- If you don''t understand an instruction, ask: *"Could you clarify what you mean by [X]? I want to make sure I do this correctly."*\n- Don''t promise what you can''t deliver — rather say *"I''ll check and confirm by end of day"* than a guess\n\n---\n\n### Difficult conversations\n\nAt some point, you will need to raise an issue with a colleague or manager. Here''s a structure that works:\n\n1. **Request** a private moment: *"Do you have 5 minutes? I''d like to raise something."*\n2. **Describe** the situation factually, not emotionally: *"When X happened, the result was Y."*\n3. **Express** the impact: *"It made it difficult for me to..."*\n4. **Request** a change or solution: *"Going forward, would it be possible to..."*\n5. **Listen** — give them space to respond without interrupting\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Clear, professional emails — reply within 24 hours, one main point per message\n✅ Match your tone to the communication channel and your relationship\n✅ Contribute in meetings — silence makes you invisible\n✅ Use a structured approach for difficult conversations'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m4', 'Time Management and Reliability', 2, 'reading', 11, true,
E'## Time Management and Reliability\n\nIn the workplace, reliability is one of the most valued qualities an employee can have — arguably more valuable than raw talent. An employer with a reliable average employee will always prefer them over a brilliant but unpredictable one.\n\n---\n\n### What "being professional" actually looks like day to day\n\n- You arrive on time, every time (or communicate in advance when you can''t)\n- You meet your deadlines or give early warning when you won''t\n- You respond to messages within a reasonable timeframe\n- You do what you said you would do\n- You don''t make excuses — you take ownership when you make mistakes\n\nThese seem obvious but they separate average employees from excellent ones. Many people know what professionalism looks like and still don''t consistently practise it.\n\n---\n\n### Why time management matters more than ever\n\nWIL placements and entry-level jobs often involve juggling multiple responsibilities across different projects and managers. Without a system, things slip.\n\nThe good news: you don''t need a complex system. You need a simple, consistent one.\n\n---\n\n### The daily priorities method\n\nEach morning (or the night before), write down:\n\n**3 MUST-DOs** — tasks that must be completed today no matter what\n**3 SHOULD-DOs** — important but not urgent; do if MUSTs are done\n**3 NICE-TO-DOs** — background tasks or longer-term projects\n\nStart with the hardest MUST-DO first. Psychologists call this "eating the frog" — getting your most uncomfortable task done early when your energy is highest.\n\n---\n\n### The "two-minute rule"\n\nIf something takes less than two minutes to do — an email reply, a quick task, filing a document — do it immediately instead of adding it to a list. Small tasks pile up faster than you think.\n\n---\n\n### Managing deadlines proactively\n\nDeadlines get missed for two main reasons:\n1. Not writing them down\n2. Starting too late\n\n**The 50% rule:** When you receive a deadline, do 50% of the work in the first half of the available time. If a report is due Friday, have a draft by Wednesday. This leaves buffer for complications and feedback.\n\nWhen you''re going to miss a deadline:\n- Tell your manager or supervisor as soon as you know — not at deadline time\n- Explain why briefly and honestly\n- Provide a new realistic completion date\n\n*"Hi — I wanted to flag that the report I was meant to submit by Friday is taking longer than expected because of [reason]. I can have it to you by Monday morning. Is that workable?"*\n\nThis is far better than submitting late without warning or submitting rushed work that''s full of errors.\n\n---\n\n### Your phone — your biggest productivity challenge\n\nResearch shows the average person checks their phone over 100 times per day. In a workplace, this is a serious problem — not just for your output but for your professional image.\n\n**Strategies:**\n- Set phone to Do Not Disturb during deep work periods\n- Keep phone off the desk during meetings\n- Use phone for work tasks only during work hours (checking personal social media included)\n- Turn off non-essential notifications\n\n---\n\n### Tools that help\n\n- **Google Calendar** — block time for deep work, not just meetings\n- **Google Tasks / Todoist** — simple task lists that sync across devices\n- **Notion / Trello** — for managing larger projects\n- **Pomodoro Technique** — work 25 minutes, break 5 minutes (free apps available)\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Reliability — doing what you said, when you said — is more valued than raw talent\n✅ Write down your top 3 must-do tasks every morning\n✅ Use the 50% rule: start early and have a draft halfway through your available time\n✅ Communicate early when a deadline is at risk — never silently miss one'),

(v_course_id, 'wwr-m4', 'Teamwork, Ethics, and Your Professional Reputation', 3, 'reading', 12, true,
E'## Teamwork, Ethics, and Your Professional Reputation\n\nYour professional reputation is built one interaction at a time — and it follows you throughout your career. In this final lesson, we cover how to show up well in a team, maintain ethical standards, and protect the reputation you''re building.\n\n---\n\n### What makes someone a great team member\n\nWhen managers are asked what they value most in entry-level employees, they rarely mention technical skills first. They say:\n\n- **Attitude** — willing to learn, takes feedback without defensiveness\n- **Dependability** — follows through; doesn''t have to be chased\n- **Communication** — keeps people informed; raises issues early\n- **Collaboration** — shares credit; doesn''t hoard information\n- **Positivity** — doesn''t complain loudly or create conflict\n\nThese are all behaviours. They can all be practised and developed.\n\n---\n\n### Receiving feedback professionally\n\nFeedback — especially critical feedback — is uncomfortable. How you receive it will define your reputation more than almost anything else.\n\n**When someone gives you critical feedback:**\n\n✅ Listen fully without interrupting\n✅ Thank them: *"I appreciate you telling me this."\n✅ Ask a clarifying question if needed: *"Could you give me an example?"\n✅ Reflect before responding: *"I''ll think about how I can improve on this."\n✅ Act on it — this is the part most people skip\n\n❌ Don''t get defensive or explain why they''re wrong\n❌ Don''t sulk, go quiet, or become hostile\n❌ Don''t discuss the feedback negatively with other colleagues\n\n---\n\n### Workplace ethics: drawing clear lines\n\n**Confidentiality**\nInformation you encounter at work — client details, internal financials, staff matters — stays internal. Do not share it externally or post about it on social media. This applies during and after employment.\n\n**Honesty**\nIf you make a mistake, own it early. Covering up mistakes almost always makes them worse. A manager who discovers you hid a problem will lose more trust than if you''d come forward immediately.\n\n**Social media**\nAssume your employer can and will see what you post. Do not post:\n- Negative comments about your employer, colleagues, or clients\n- Photos or videos from the workplace without permission\n- Company information, documents, or data\n\n**Theft — including time theft**\nTime theft — being paid for hours you didn''t work, leaving early without authorisation, doing personal tasks on work time — is one of the most common ethical violations in workplaces. Treat it as seriously as any other form of theft.\n\n---\n\n### Navigating difficult situations\n\n**If asked to do something unethical:**\nSay: *"I''m not comfortable with that — is there a different approach we could take?"* Most ethical violations are preventable if you speak up early.\n\n**If you witness misconduct:**\nMost companies have a confidential reporting mechanism. Use it. Staying silent makes you complicit.\n\n**If you feel harassed or discriminated against:**\nDocument incidents (date, what was said or done, who witnessed it) and report to HR. You have rights under the Labour Relations Act and the Employment Equity Act.\n\n---\n\n### Building your professional reputation from day one\n\nYour workplace reputation is built on:\n1. **Consistency** — showing up the same way every day\n2. **Excellence in small things** — people who do ordinary tasks extraordinarily well get noticed\n3. **Generosity** — offering to help, sharing knowledge, supporting colleagues\n4. **Discretion** — not gossiping, not oversharing, not complaining publicly\n5. **Ambition with humility** — showing you want to grow while respecting that you have much to learn\n\n> *"Your reputation is built before you have a title. The habits you build now are the habits that will define your career."*\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n✅ Attitude, dependability, and communication matter more than technical skill at entry level\n✅ Receive feedback without defensiveness — and then act on it\n✅ Maintain strict confidentiality about everything you encounter at work\n✅ Your professional reputation is built one interaction at a time — start building it now');

